 "drug_rule": "Cyclobenzaprine is a tricyclic muscle relaxant that blocks histamine H1 and muscarinic receptors, causing sedation and anticholinergic effects. Buspirone acts on 5-HT1A receptors, producing mild sedation and anxiolysis. Co-administration leads to additive CNS depression. Cyclobenzaprine also has weak anticholinergic and antihistaminic properties that can exacerbate buspirone's sedative profile. The combination is contraindicated or strongly cautioned against in drug interaction databases due to increased risk of excessive drowsiness, falls, and cognitive impairment.\n\n###",
 "patient_fit": "Although each drug is individually appropriate for its indication (cyclobenzaprine for muscle spasms, buspirone for GAD), the patient's profile - specifically the concurrent use of two CNS-depressant agents - overrides general safety assumptions. The additive sedative effect and potential for QT prolongation make this combination unsafe, even though the patient has no pre-existing cardiac impairment. The verdict of \"unsafe\" is therefore justified.\n\n###",
